  "text": "Sure! Let's write a function called `word_histogram` that does the following:\n\n1. Converts the input string to **lowercase** to **ignore case**.\n2. **Removes punctuation** from the string.\n3. Splits the string into words.\n4. Counts the **frequency** of each word using a dictionary.\n\nHere's the Python function:\n\n```python\nimport string\n\ndef word_histogram(text):\n    # Convert to lowercase\n    text = text.lower()\n    \n    # Remove punctuation\n    text = text.translate(str.maketrans('', '', string.punctuation))\n    \n    # Split into words\n    words = text.split()\n    \n    # Count frequencies\n    histogram = {}\n    for word in words:\n        if word in histogram:\n            histogram[word] += 1\n        else:\n            histogram[word] = 1\n    \n    return histogram\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n\n- `string.punctuation` gives all punctuation characters: `!\"#$%&'()*+,-./:;<=>?@[\\]^_`{|}~`\n- `str.maketrans('', '', string.punctuation)` creates a translation table to remove all punctuation.\n- `text.translate(...)` removes the punctuation.\n- `text.split()` splits the string into words (split by whitespace).\n- We loop through the words and build a dictionary with word frequencies.\n\n### Testing the Function:\n\n```python\nassert word_histogram(\"Hello, world! Hello.\") == {\"hello\": 2, \"world\": 1}\nassert word_histogram(\"Python is great. Python is fun!\") == {\"python\": 2, \"is\": 2, \"great\": 1, \"fun\": 1}\nassert word_histogram(\"Test, test, TEST.\") == {\"test\": 3}\n```\n\nAll assertions should pass if the function is implemented correctly. Let me know if you need it in another language or want it to handle more edge cases!",
